        "math_academy_id": "q-110279",
        "problem": "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(5)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $5$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |",
        "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(5)=\\frac{g(5)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(5))}+{g}^{\u2032}(5){f}^{-1}(5).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=-1,$ $g(5)=-4,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(5)=1,$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(-1)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(5) & =\\frac{g(5)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(5))}+{g}^{\u2032}(5){f}^{-1}(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{{f}^{\u2032}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-5}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
